      "documentSlug": "music-art-and-creative-therapies-in-pd",
      "documentConcept": "Music, art and creative therapies in PD",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:b663d41f42f7c4ed658f24e883258fd6a66715398e9f8c7c0300122585581240",
      "text": "Educational summary of what published sources say, not medical advice. Meta-analyses of rhythmic auditory stimulation and music-based interventions in Parkinson's disease report positive pooled effects on gait velocity and stride length but not cadence, and disagree on balance, timed up-and-go, UPDRS motor scores and freezing of gait. One singing review rates the certainty of its RCT-based finding as very low and another calls singing a promising adjunct therapy, and a trial's own authors limit attribution to singing. Reviews conflict on quality of life, mood and cognition. The visual art therapy evidence cited here is an exploratory observational study, and a meta-analysis calls visual arts and painting a research gap; one art-therapy meta-analysis of 26 trials included 14 dance trials. Review authors repeatedly flag small samples and limited blinding.",
